<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Nore</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Nore is a river of south-east Ireland, about 140 km long, rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-tipperary">County Tipperary</a> and flowing through Laois and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a> to join the Barrow above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-ross">New Ross</a>. With the Barrow and the Suir it forms the 'Three Sisters' river system draining into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> Harbour. Its fertile valley was the heartland of the early medieval kingdom of Osraige, and the city of Kilkenny stands on its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-nore.jpg" alt="River Nore" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sheila1988 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bridge_on_the_Nore._Kilkenny.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
